\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia moves on rupiah with Singapore, Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 3 (Reuters) - Indonesian Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad said Bank Indonesia had acted in concert with Singapore and Japan on Monday to support the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  "Bank Indonesia today acted in the local market, together with both the Bank of Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore in support of the Indonesian rupiah," he said in a statement obtained by Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "Today's concerted effort was aimed at strengthening the rupiah to levels more appropriate with the fundamentals of the Indonesian economy," Mar'ie said. \par \par \uc2  "The three authorities also confirmed that they stand ready to take steps necessary in the future to maintain stability of the rupiah as a part of stabilising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region." \par \par \uc2  Mar'ie said this effort would contribute positively to the implementation of the assistance package supported by the International Monetary Fund (IMF), the World Bank and the Asian Development Bank. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministries of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan had said earlier that they had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to show confidence in the macroeconomic policies of the Indonesian government. \par \par \uc2  -- Jakarta newsroom (6221) 384-6364; Fax (6221) 344-8404 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb303ecs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
