\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  surge on rupiah rally. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 932 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 1997\par 06:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 3 (Reuters) - Most Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged on Monday as Indonesia's early efforts to reform its economy in response to a massive IMF-led international aid package put a shine on the region. \par \par \uc2  A more upbeat mood on most Asian stock markets, particularly Hong Kong and Singapore, also boosted regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah shot to a high of 3,255 to the dollar from an opening around 3,540/55 on talk of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by foreign central banks. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers attributed the scale of the rupiah's rise to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan to sell dollars through the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS). \par \par \uc2  They said confirmation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japanese Finance Ministries gave the rupiah another push. \par \par \uc2  In separate statements released in Singapore, the ministries said the Bank of Japan (BOJ) and \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in concert with Bank Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Finance Minister Richard Hu said in the statement the MAS bought Indonesian rupiah against the U.S. dollar in concert with the other authorities. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's participation in this joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflects our confidence in the macroeconomic policies of the Indonesian government," it said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka said in a statement the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "aimed at correcting excessive depreciation of the Indonesian rupiah," and the three authorities would be ready to act again when necessary. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupiah was also lifted by Indonesia's weekend move to liquidate 16 unhealthy banks, reflecting a keen desire to meet an IMF-backed programme to restore its economy. \par \par \uc2  "Indonesia has helped a lot. They've taken firm actions, unlike the Thais," a European bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  In August, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) organised $17.2 billion in aid for Thailand and on Monday the Thai baht was the only Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall, plagued by political uncertainties and delays in implementing financial sector reforms. \par \par \uc2  The baht was at 41.050/180 to the dollar onshore against 40.70/41.00 late on Friday. But it firmed to 39.92/40.12 offshore against 40.60/90 previously. \par \par \uc2  "Corporates and importers bought actively as they are not sure where the baht would go given political uncertainties," a local bank dealer in Bangkok said. \par \par \uc2  "Moreover, once the baht hit the 40.00 level, it generated a lot of doubts over the government's ability to handle the economy," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Other dealers said the baht's drop reflected both genuine commercial dollar demand and speculative buying. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kosit Panpiemras urged Thais to refrain from buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless absolutely necessary. \par \par \uc2  He also said he was confident of the long-term stability of the baht, which has plunged to new lows in recent days, if IMF guidelines were followed. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was sharply higher at 1.5530/66 to the U.S. dollar at 0500 GMT against an opening below 1.5740. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's benchmark stock index ended the morning 5.84 percent higher at 1,678.72 points. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like dollar/Sing has seen a top at 1.5970. If it rallies to 1.5700/40 now, I'd favour a sell," another European bank dealer said. She said the U.S. dollar's breach of the 1.5740 technical support would limit its upside. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit surged to 3.2550/650 to the dollar from 3.4070/180 late on Friday, as players focused on the rupiah's gains. A Singapore dealer said a large oil company was heard to be a big ringgit buyer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said funds were likely to take profits on their long dollar positions after U.S. financier George Soros said there was a need for more market regulation, echoing Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad's now familiar refrain. \par \par \uc2  Soros told the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC) on Saturday that recent market turmoil showed the need for central banks and regulators to band together to rein in speculators. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir has repeatedly accused Soros of taking positions against the ringgit and intensifying its drop. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was firm at 35.15/25 to the dollar against Friday's 35.42 close as the market took its cue from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's offer price of 35.40 to 35.20 pesos. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, the Taiwan dollar ploughed new depths of T$31.240 to the U.S. dollar amid widespread expectations of long-term depreciation as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained out of view. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won remained depressed at 966.50/9.80 to the dollar despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell about $300 million at 965 won. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in again at the 970 won level. \par \par \uc2  A Bank of Korea official told Reuters on Saturday the battered won would "never, never, never" breach the psychological 1,000 to the dollar level. \par \par \uc2  "That was a dangerous statement. It immediately made the won go to 970 today: never say never," said a Singapore dealer. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was steady at 7.7325/45 to the U.S. dollar as forwards eased and interbank rates came off. Dealers said the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not seen adding liquidity to the market after last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar received a boost from a firm set of retail sales data, swinging sentiment against a rate cut. It firmed to $0.7125/35 from an early $0.7007/12. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb303ecm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
