\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  hold up as bourses dive. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 1,035 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1997\par 10:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held their ground for most of Tuesday as the U.S. dollar fell out of favour after Wall Street's historic drop overnight. \par \par \uc2  Wide spreads and thin volumes exaggerated volatility, but dealers said most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept their cool after an expected sell-off on the back of diving regional stock markets failed to materialise. \par \par \uc2  Still, there was little respite for frightened investors as stock markets from Karachi to Sydney reeled from the impact of the Dow's 554-point drop, its biggest single-day point loss. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index, which sparked mayhem in global stock markets last week, ended the day almost 14 percent down after hitting a 26-month low of 8,775.88 while other Asia-Pacific bourses posted drops of between four and 12 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, fragile after the Dow's plunge, fell sharply against the mark and yen during Asian hours, prompting players to reduce their dollar holdings against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  too. \par \par \uc2  "Instead of selling regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the fall-off in the stock markets, people were buying them and selling the U.S. dollar instead because the Dow has fallen," said Jacqueline Ong, regional economist at research firm I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  "But we're not sure whether this will be sustainable. We see more pressure building up," she added. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was at 3.4150/250 to the U.S. dollar at 0930 GMT after diving to a record low of 3.4600 earlier. \par \par \uc2  Talk that a large Singapore investment house had recommended buying the ringgit below the 3.40 to the dollar level helped prop up the ringgit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There were good names selling dollar/ringgit at 3.40/41. I think the market's long of dollars," a European bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the topside in the dollar/regionals has been seen. I would prefer to sell on rallies," she added. \par \par \uc2  But others said the ringgit remained vulnerable to further weakness towards the 3.50 level. The benchmark Kuala Lumpur Composite Index ended down 6.64 percent to 647.32. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won skidded through a series of record lows, closing at 957.60 to the dollar, the day's lowest permitted level and nearly two percent off Zits previous close at 939.90. \par \par \uc2  Pressure on the won mounted after Moody's Investors Service downgraded Seoul's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ceiling for short-term debt. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hands-off stance after it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in spot and forward markets early, to no avail, exacerbated the won's woes, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ended at a 10-year low of T$30.80 against Monday's T$30.510 as players were spooked by stock market sales. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended slightly higher at 35.15 to the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars through the day. \par \par \uc2  Manila traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts helped shield the peso from the stock market's traumas. The main share index lost 6.31 percent to end at a new four-year low of 1,740.18. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gabriel Singson said the bank had asked commercial banks to cooperate in curbing speculation against the peso and to look at ways to lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Philippines Finance Secretary Roberto de Ocampo said there was no need to panic over the fall in Asian stock markets, describing it as a "corrective" drop, not a long-term trend. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah recovered to 3,610/20 to the dollar from an early low of 3,775. Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen checking prices and some said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  selectively to smooth rates as it had did in the past few days. \par \par \uc2  Moody's downgrade of the outlook for Indonesia's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  country ceiling to negative from stable had no sustained impact as players awaited news on an IMF aid package. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was at 1.5780/95 to the U.S. dollar after touching a low of 1.5950. Dealers said there was talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the forward market, supporting three-month swaps. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the MAS is expecting and also accepting a weaker Singapore dollar. I wouldn't be surprised to see it at 1.60 or even 1.62 to the U.S. dollar," a local bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Singapore dollar was helped by a newspaper report that one of Singapore's Big Four banks, Overseas Union Bank (OUB), had raised short-term deposit rates by as much as two percentage points. \par \par \uc2  An OUB spokeswoman told Reuters the bank had not changed the board rates for any of its Singapore dollar deposits. She said the rate rise for short term deposits mentioned in the newspaper report "was a special quotation for a client for a special amount". \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht slipped to 39.10/20 to the dollar onshore against 38.45/38.55 late on Monday as importers sought dollars while exporters held back from selling them in the face of the market turmoil. The baht was at 38.95/05 to the dollar offshore. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar dipped to 7.7310/50 to the U.S. dollar from 7.7285/35 five hours earlier as interest rates eased after the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) added liquidity to the interbank system to counter pressure on the stock market. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the HKMA offered overnight money at six percent, one-week money at 12 percent and three-month at 13.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa said his government was optimistic about the stock market, blaming Tuesday's drop on plunging overseas markets. He also said the Hong Kong dollar was likely to stabilise as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators had retreated. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar pulled back from a 46-month low of US$0.6820 as rumours of Reserve Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnerved a very short market. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie dollar's rebound helped the New Zealand dollar recover from a three-year low of 0.6145. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtas03nad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
