\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  jumpy as bourses crumble. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 950 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1997\par 06:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  clambered off their early lows on Tuesday, but dealers said the mood remained very jumpy as stock markets around the region crumpled following Wall Street's historic drop overnight. \par \par \uc2  Volatility was exaggerated by wide spreads and thin volumes as traders kept a wary eye on activity in the stock markets, where there was no sign of respite for frightened investors. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's blue chip Hang Seng Index, which sparked mayhem in global stock markets last week, ended the morning down 15.45 percent at 8,876.40 while other Asia-Pacific bourses posted drops of between four and 12 percent. \par \par \uc2  But most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  regained some poise by midday as an expected sell-off on the back of stock market losses failed to materialise. \par \par \uc2  "Instead of selling regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the fall-off in the stock markets, people were buying them and selling the U.S. dollar instead because the Dow has fallen," said Jacqueline Ong, regional economist at research firm I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  "But we're not sure whether this will be sustainable. We see more pressure building up," she added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, fragile after the Dow's 554-point drop, lost further ground against the yen and mark in Tokyo trade with a slew of stop-loss dollar sales triggered at several points. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit recovered to around 3.3950/50 to the U.S. dollar at 0450 GMT after diving to a record low of 3.4600 in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Talk that a large Singapore investment house had recommended buying the ringgit below the 3.40 to the dollar level helped the rebound, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But they said the ringgit remained vulnerable to further weakness towards the 3.50 level. The benchmark Kuala Lumpur Composite Index extended its early decline, ending the morning down 6.07 percent to 651.30. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won skidded through a series of record lows, falling as far as 955.00 to the dollar, down sharply from its previous close at 939.90. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won was likely to fall further as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed out of the market after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the spot and forward markets earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso also remained bruised at 35.30/33 to the dollar, but its falls were cushioned by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early dollar sales at the 35.20 pesos level. \par \par \uc2  Manila traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later raised its offer rate for the dollar to 35.50 pesos, helping shield the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the stock market's traumas. The main share index lost 6.31 percent to end at a new four-year low of 1,740.18. \par \par \uc2  Philippines Finance Secretary Roberto de Ocampo said there was no need to panic over the fall in Asian stock markets, describing it as a "corrective" drop, not a long-term trend. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah recovered to 3,630/40 to the dollar from an early low of 3,775. Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen checking prices and some said the bank was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  selectively to smooth rates as it had did in the past few days. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was knocked off its highs after Moody's Investors Service revised the outlook for Indonesia's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  country ceiling to negative from stable due to the effects of the rupiah's depreciation on the government's fiscal position. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was at 1.5755/85 to the U.S. dollar after touching a low of 1.5950. Dealers said there was talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore had checked rates but they had not detected any actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was helped by a newspaper report that one of Singapore's Big Four banks, Overseas Union Bank (OUB), had raised short-term deposit rates by as much as two percentage points. \par \par \uc2  An OUB spokeswoman told Reuters the bank had not changed the board rates for any of its Singapore dollar deposits. She said the rate rise for short term deposits mentioned in the newspaper report "was a special quotation for a client for a special amount". \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht slipped to 38.90/95 to the dollar onshore against 38.45/38.55 late on Monday as importers sought dollars while exporters held back from selling them in the face of the market turmoil. The baht was at 38.50/70 to the dollar offshore after falling as low as 39.20. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was relatively steady at 7.7285/35 to the U.S. dollar as the territory's Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa held his first crisis cabinet meeting amid growing carnage in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  So far, Tung and his senior officials have said they would pay the price of substantially higher interest rates to defend the Hong Kong dollar against speculative attacks. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen adding liquidity to the interbank system on Tuesday in a bid to ease interest rates as the stock market tumbled. Overnight rates eased to six percent from Monday's seven percent, a far cry from last week's highs of 300 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar pulled back from a 46-month low of US$0.6820 as rumours of Reserve Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnerved a very short market. \par \par \uc2  Reserve Bank deputy governor Graeme Thompson said earlier the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was closely watching the strife in Asian financial markets, but he declined to comment further. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie dollar's rebound helped the New Zealand dollar recover to US$0.6173/80 from a three-year low of 0.6145. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtas03n8n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
