\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  shaky as market looks north. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 1997\par 06:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 27 (Reuters) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trickled lower in subdued trade on Monday as North Asia's nervous markets hogged the limelight. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said players remained concerned about a fresh speculative assault on the Hong Kong dollar despite its relative stability and lower overnight interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was at 7.7280/30 to the U.S. dollar at 0445 GMT against 7.7280/90 late on Friday. The overnight rate was at seven percent compared with Friday's high of 50 percent. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index ended the morning down six percent at 10,475.53 as nervous investors unloaded holdings after Wall Street failed to take its cue from a recovery in global stock markets on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The market seems quite orderly this morning, but I think funds still want to attack the Hong Kong dollar. Look at what's happening to the Korean won," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The beleaguered won dived to a record low of 942.00 to the dollar as heavy speculative dollar buying overwhelmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early efforts to stem the won's fall by selling dollars at 929.90 won, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The won was at 940.10/20 against Friday's close at 929.50. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said worries about corporate debt defaults and foreign fund outflows from the stock market were fuelling dollar buying. The composite stock index was down 2.01 percent to a year low of 537.43. \par \par \uc2  South Korean President Kim Young-sam urged economic ministers to introduce measures to stabilise the financial markets. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the staid Singapore dollar saw the biggest move, hitting an early low of 1.5920 to the U.S. dollar after opening near its late Friday levels of 1.5780. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the drop, driven by U.S. hedge funds, to bullish sentiment for the U.S. dollar and weekend reports quoting Singapore leaders. \par \par \uc2  Senior Minister Lee Kuan Yew was quoted as saying Singapore had allowed its dollar to go down and wanted it to be at a competitive level. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Richard Hu was quoted as saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market occasionally to prevent wild fluctuations, but lets the market determine the Singapore dollar's level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said there was nothing new about the comments but they gave players an excuse to buy the U.S. dollar. "I don't know why the market took this as an excuse, but they're basically quite bullish on dollar/Sing for the time being, ready to take it to 1.60," the U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was at 1.5865/85 at 0445 GMT, off its recent low of 1.5970 seen last week. \par \par \uc2  "The bias remains on the upside for dollar/Sing unless the regionals make a strong recovery, and also the Hong Kong dollar," said Alison Seng, an analyst as MMS Standard & Poor's. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was steady at 38.45/55 to the dollar onshore and 38.62/72 offshore following the appointment of a new Finance Minister, Kosit Panpiemras. \par \par \uc2  The market largely shrugged off Kosit's promises to try to stop the economy from sliding further by maintaining strict fiscal discipline. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the dollar's upside looked capped for the time being as the market welcomed the removal of some political uncertainty and exporters were keen to sell dollars at 39 baht. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was weak at 3.3850/900 to the dollar against 3.3700/800 late on Friday, but a lack of fresh factors and fears of dollar sales by Bank Negara's agent banks limited its falls. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was wedged between Jakarta banks' dollar orders around 3,560 rupiah and offers near 3,590/600, as players continued their wait for an announcement about the IMF's assistance package for Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad was quoted by the Kompas newspaper as saying he could not say when negotiations would be completed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the underlying tone remained biddish for dollars but the IMF package and the threat of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  slowed demand and kept the market cautious. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso recovered to 35.150/350 to the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars at 35.50 pesos in early trade to curb pressure on the peso. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtar03l4n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
