\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  cold to Hong Kong bounce. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 905 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 1997\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 24 (Reuters) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained downtrodden on Friday as most regional stock markets failed to follow Hong Kong's startling recovery with the same enthusiasm they showed for its descent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar maintained its favoured position against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but its rise was limited by pre-weekend position squaring in a generally calmer market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index ended almost seven percent up at 11,144.34 points, reversing an early drop and regaining some poise after Thursday's historic losses shook markets worldwide. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar steered clear of the 7.75 level to the U.S. dollar, believed to be a trigger point for official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was at 7.7260/10 to the U.S. dollar at 0945 GMT against 7.7065/15 late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Overnight rates slumped to a week low of six percent from Thursday's highs of 300 percent after the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) was seen lending overnight money aggressively. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa voiced confidence in winning the war against speculators. \par \par \uc2  "We are determined to defend the dollar peg," Tung said after returning to Hong Kong from a visit to Europe. "We are trying to beat off speculative attacks on our dollar. We will succeed in that." \par \par \uc2  He said interest rates would remain high only in the short term. The Hong Kong dollar jumped on Thursday as a liquidity squeeze, believed to have been engineered by the HKMA, sent short-term interest rates soaring. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts remained divided on the sustainability of the Hong Kong dollar's link to the U.S. dollar, but most agreed that a complete removal of the peg would further shake confidence in fragile Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  "They may shift it a bit to 8.00 or 8.20 to the U.S. dollar but they won't give it up completely. It's done well for them," said a senior regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Singapore bank. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's peg to the U.S. dollar has been held steady at 7.80 since the link was established in 1983. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, overnight interest rates came off Thursday's heady levels of 50 percent but remained relatively firm at 10-15 percent. Dealers said there was no sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot or money markets after Friday's spike up in short-term rates triggered speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was at 1.5800/20 to the U.S. dollar after swinging between a high of 1.5675 and a low of 1.5835. Dealers expected it to head for the long-awaited 1.60 level next week, alongside renewed selling of other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit had an even more volatile day, hitting an early record low of 3.4330 to the dollar and later firming to 3.3300 on pre-weekend covering. It was at 3.3725/75 to the U.S. dollar at 0945 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the early drop to thin market volumes and vague talk that Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad had said Malaysians would have to tolerate a dollar/ringgit rate of 3.60. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's rise was capped by fears of dollar sales by agent banks of Bank Negara Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  The baht slid as Thai markets resumed trading after a holiday that shielded them from the worst of the fallout from Hong Kong on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But political uncertainties and Standard and Poor's downward revision of Thailand's long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and baht-denominated debt ratings kept the baht heavy at 38.50/60 to the dollar onshore against 37.95/38.25 late on Wednesday. It was at 38.80/90 offshore against Thursday's 37.65. \par \par \uc2  The market was awaiting the announcement of Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh's new coalition cabinet, but sceptical that it would be able to cure the country's economic ills. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah firmed in late trade after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars to take advantage of the momentum from dollar sales by offshore funds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was at 3,575/60 but trade was subdued as players remained on tenterhooks for details of an IMF-led assistance package. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sudradjad Djiwandono said talks with the IMF-led team continued on Friday and an announcement would be made once they were concluded. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won ended weak at 929.50 to the dollar after hitting a record low of 930.00 despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to prop it up. Dealers blamed the won's drop on heavy foreign selling of stocks, which saw the composite index down 5.49 percent to 570.91. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ended at T$30.362 after hitting a low of T$30.550, triggering apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The market earlier ignored \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sheu Yuan-dong's comment that the Taiwan dollar would rise in the mid-term and would be "reasonable" at T$28.5-T$28.6. \par \par \uc2  "What \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is he talking about?" asked one dealer. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said foreign reserves fell to US$85.752 billion at end September from US$87.793 billion a month before, mainly due to the bank's massive defence of the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtao03ujd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
