\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Stock market blues hit regionals. \b0\par\par\uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 23 (Reuters) - Most Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stumbled lower on Thursday as Hong Kong's collapsing stock market triggered shock waves across most of Asia. \par \par \uc2  Declines in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were led largely by funds fleeing regional stock markets in the face of enduring pessimism about Asia's prospects for a recovery, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The normally resilient Singapore dollar led the fray, dropping to a 46-month low of 1.5970 to the U.S. dollar in morning trade before talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) pushed it back up to 1.5850. \par \par \uc2  It firmed further to 1.5740/70 at 0450 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The Singapore stock market was down nearly 100 pips so a lot of funds were pulling out of the Sing dollar," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But then the overnight interest rate went as high as 50 percent and dollar/Sing collapsed all the way from 1.5950 to 1.5860," he said. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Singapore stock index ended the morning session down 5.43 percent at 1,637.66. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the MAS was probably behind the money market liquidity squeeze, jacking up short-term rates and making it painfully expensive to run a short position. \par \par \uc2  "Local banks were offering U.S. dollars on the way up above the 1.5950/60 level, but it's more the squeeze in liquidity that's doing the job," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  He said the Singapore dollar could strengthen towards 1.57 to the U.S. dollar, and possibly head higher in the near term, as the interbank market had been caught long. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the Singapore dollar was likely to head towards 1.60 and possibly 1.65 in the not-too-distant future. \par \par \uc2  "People realise that Singapore cannot escape the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil in the region and it may take another three to four months to settle," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was held hostage to its neighbour's fortunes, sliding to 3.4150 to the dollar before recovering to around 3.3550/650. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. funds are still buying the dollar. The retracement was shallow, so we should see dollar/Malay testing new highs," a European bank dealer in Singapore said. "It may try 3.45/46 first, but people are already looking at 3.50-3.60." \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's diving stock market and a U.S. investment bank's recommendation to steer clear of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also did not bode well for the ringgit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Sun Bae Kim, senior international economist at Goldman Sachs, said on Wednesday that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility was likely to continue over the near term and advised investors to refrain from "bottom fishing" despite the oversold situation. \par \par \uc2  The key Kuala Lumpur stock index was down 4.70 percent to 696.78 at the midday break. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars at 34.50 pesos to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the local stock market lost over four percent. \par \par \uc2  Manila traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have sold about $10 million, taking advantage of a market that was long on dollars. The peso firmed to 34.35/55 against an opening 34.73. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah continued to hold its ground in the face of mayhem elsewhere, benefitting initially from a rumour that Indonesia would receive massive financial assistance from the International Monetary Fund (IMF). \par \par \uc2  "There were rumours that the IMF would extend $25 billion to Indonesia," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta dealers said the rupiah was also boosted early by overnight U.S. fund buying. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was at 3,640/50, little changed from its late Wednesday levels of 3,660/70. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was subdued at 38.990/39.090 to the dollar in offshore markets against 38.45/55 late on Wednesday. Thai markets were closed for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar, blamed for the latest crisis in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, rose to 7.6100/50 per U.S. dollar from an opening at 7.7410/20. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) was squeezing liquidity to ward off speculative attacks on Asia's last U.S. dollar-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the stock market went into a free-fall, losing over 14 percent at one point. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index ended the morning down 9.35 percent at 10,549.47 points. \par \par \uc2  Overnight rates shot to 250 to 300 percent by noon, compared with about 6.0 percent on Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA also said it might levy penal rates on a case-by-case basis to discourage repeated borrowings from its liquidity adjustment facility. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar shed its early gains, slipping to T$30.350/398 against Wednesday's T$30.211 close. \par \par \uc2  -- Syed Azman in Kuala Lumpur (603 201-4813), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221 384-6364), Lilian Kurunungan in Manila (632-841-8938), Sean Kennedy in Hong Kong (852-2847-4043), Taipei newsroom (886-2508-0815) and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703090) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtan03ox3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
