\par\par \b \uc2  Focus-Thai worries weigh on SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 1997\par 12:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 22 (Reuters) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were pummelled to fresh lows on Wednesday as Thailand's mounting political crisis looked set to throttle prospects of an economic recovery before it could even get off the ground. \par \par \uc2  Even the Hong Kong dollar, which has Asia's strongest link to the U.S. dollar, faced growing pressure despite efforts by the territory's officials to shore up confidence. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, street protests demanding the resignation of Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh entered their third day as the baht tumbled towards the 40 level to the dollar in both domestic and offshore markets. \par \par \uc2  It recovered to 38.05/30 per dollar onshore and 38.50/60 offshore at 1015 GMT from lows of 39.80 and 39.90 earlier, partly due to market talk that a favourable candidate had agreed to accept the finance ministry portfolio. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Thanong Bidaya's decision to resign after an impending cabinet reshuffle had prompted concern Thailand might not meet the conditions for a $17.2 billion loan package arranged by the International Monetary Fund (IMF), dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand governor Chaiyawat Wibulswasdi attributed the sell-off in the baht to political factors and said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would let the market determine its level. \par \par \uc2  The cabinet reshuffle is expected to be finalised this week and should show some major changes, a minister with the powerful Chart Pattana Party told Reuters on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht's decline might be limited for the time being, but the market remained determined to test the 40 level and possibly push the baht lower. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit also caved in to worries about Thailand and lingering disappointment with Malaysia's efforts to address its problems in its 1998 budget. \par \par \uc2  It skidded to 3.43 to the dollar, eclipsing its previous record low of 3.4080, but later recovered to 3.3880/980. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have been checking rates when the ringgit fell through 3.40 and Kuala Lumpur banks were active dollar sellers above 3.41/42. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar slithered to a fresh 42-month low of 1.5885 to the U.S. dollar, over two percent below its Tuesday low of 1.5740. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the haemorrhaging stock market did not help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the benchmark index falling 2.23 percent to close at a new year low of 1,731.68. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was believed to have checked rates and possibly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  sporadically, but this only smoothed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's decline. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped pull the rupiah back to 3,645/55 to the dollar from a day low of 3,700. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was holding up relatively well as players focused on the outcome of talks between Indonesian authorities and the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Further north, the Hong Kong dollar slipped to 7.7497/02 to the dollar from 7.7475/85 late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Fierce selling of Hong Kong stocks did little to bolster sentiment as the blue chip Hang Seng Index lost a whopping 765.33 points or 6.17 percent to end at 11,637.77, its lowest in 13 months. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was backed by huge foreign reserves and sound fundamentals, echoing Financial Secretary Donald Tsang's early comments. \par \par \uc2  Tung said Hong Kong would not pursue a competitive devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid steep declines in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that rates may have to rise in the process of defending the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers did not expect Hong Kong to surrender its 14-year peg to the U.S. dollar easily but they noted that defending it would drain reserves. \par \par \uc2  "The pressure is quite immense this time because the stock market is really performing badly...and all the countries around Hong Kong have already surrendered to this pressure," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar closed firmer at T$30.211 against T$30.615 on Tuesday as investors booked profit on the dollar's sharp rise since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped defending the Taiwan dollar last Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won was propped up by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's continuous dollar sales between 922 won and 916 won to the dollar. The won closed at 915.0 against Tuesday's 924.40. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtam03y3z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
