\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar/Sing hit by strong fund buying - dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Strong buying of the U.S. dollar against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the Singapore dollar on a roller-coaster ride on Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I see buying from the local boys, the Americans, the interbank, London parties and even Hong Kong funds. Everyone is buying the dollar against the regionals," said a dealer with a foreign institution. \par \par \uc2  At 0850 GMT, the Singapore dollar was quoted at a 42-month low of 1.5740/50. \par \par \uc2  "The market could be caught short. So there is lots of short-covering on the back of the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  troubles, especially after the baht eased," said a dealer with a local bank. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, the Sing dollar had been quite strong against the rest of the regionals. I think funds are trying to see if dollar/Sing can play catch up," said a dealer with a U.S. house. Some dealers said there could have been some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), buying the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1.5750 to a dollar. \par \par \uc2  "This cannot be confirmed. But from the way the local banks were selling the dollar against the Sing at 1.5745/50 initially and then at 1.5770/80, there seems to be some support," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market expects the Singapore dollar to test 1.60 to the U.S. within a week. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/Sing is still healthy against the rest of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Looking at the crosses, ringgit/Sing is at 0.47 but I think it would be more comfortable at 0.48/0.49," said the dealer with a local bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers mostly shrugged off the strong nominal 15.8 percent year-on-year growth in Singapore's September non-oil domestic exports announced on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "I think we are not looking at fundamentals at the moment. The market just wants to buy the dollars. And the Straits Times Industrials Index (STII) plunge of over one percent gave more momentum to that buying," said the dealer with a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  The STII ended 1.32 percent or 23.70 points down at 1,771.22 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -- Angela Tan (65) 870-3083; Fax (65) 776-8112 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtal03wh3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
