\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -2:Mkt Sees Thai Package As "Non-Event" \b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 1997\par 13:40\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "What really moved the market was the rumor that, as\par part of the package, the (Thai) government was going to lift\par the trading ban," said a trader at a Southeast Asian bank,\par referring to the exchange and capital controls imposed by\par the Bank of Thailand in May.\par    Although the package contained no mention of lifting the\par controls, market sentiment toward the baht failed to\par lighten.\par    "The package was a non-event. The market was\par disappointed there were no new ideas in it for reforming the\par economy," said the trader.\par    This view was echoed by other market participants,\par including a trader at a major U.S. bank, who said that the\par 10-year time limit imposed on foreign majority stakes in\par Thai finance companies was actively detering foreign\par companies from injecting capital into the sector.\par    In the absence of any new solutions to Thailand's\par financial crisis, or any news of the lifting of controls,\par the baht is likely to remain in a range of 36.00 baht to\par 36.50 baht to the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar, the trader said.\par    Elsewhere in the region, the Singapore dollar suffered a\par sharp fall in reaction to comments made in Hong Kong by\par Singapore Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong. Goh said the\par Singapore government is happy to let the market decide the\par value of the Singapore dollar, but added that Singapore\par could not hope to emerge unscathed from the regional\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis.\par    After reaching a low of S$1.5440 to the dollar soon\par after midday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recovered some of its lost ground\par on what traders said was aggressive selling of U.S. dollars\par by local banks. The assumption among market participants was\par that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the republic's de\par facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to check the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par fall.\par    "The MAS doesnt mind seeing the Singapore dollar\par depreciate, but it does like things to be orderly," said an\par interbank trader at a large U.S. bank in Singapore.\par    The Singapore dollar finished Asian trading at S$1.5395,\par well above its earlier low, and slightly up from S$1.5400\par late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) DOW JONES NEWS  10-14-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0740EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4340 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dtae026pk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
