\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Thai baht gleams in S.E. Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  gloom. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 1997\par 07:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 25 (Reuter) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost their footing again on Thursday as persistent demand for cheap dollars from companies and hedge funds thwarted rises. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of massive selling of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but dealers said their failure to extend the gains of the last two days prompted players to reverse track amid continued uncertainty about economic and political developments. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht alone was insulated from the region's gloomy performance, extending its recent rise as sentiment brightened after the IMF's endorsement of Bangkok's efforts at implementing reforms to help stabilise the economy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said sentiment for the baht appeared to improve markedly following the World Bank/International Monetary Fund meeting in Hong Kong this week. \par \par \uc2  "Actually there were lots of kind words and positive views about Thailand coming out of that meeting," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Bangkok. \par \par \uc2  The baht was at 35.70/80 to the dollar onshore at 0445 GMT against 36.20/30. Offshore it was at 34.85/95 against 35.15/25. \par \par \uc2  Bangkok dealers said dollar selling by offshore players in the past few days was prompted promptly by market talk that Thai authorities may remove a two-tier baht system soon. \par \par \uc2  The head of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a European bank in Singapore said dollar sales were also being fuelled by hedge funds closing outright positions taken in mid-May, when the baht first came under speculative attack. \par \par \uc2  Thailand then tried to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by separating its domestic baht market from offshore markets. \par \par \uc2  "But the underlying sentiment is still positive for the dollar. Corporates and funds will buy any dip below the 34.50 baht level," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit slid to 3.0610/60 to the dollar from 3.0400/50 late on Wednesday, but dealers said dollar selling interest from companies and hedge funds above the 3.0800 level would limit its decline for the time being. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, the dollar would be well sought on declines towards the 3.00 ringgit level after aggressive overnight buying by hedge funds near 3.02 ringgit. \par \par \uc2  The head of trading at a foreign bank in Kuala Lumpur said sentiment was also affected by rumours that finance firm MBf Capital was in financial trouble. \par \par \uc2  "I think foreigners are using MBf news to buy dollars again," he said. He said the rumour was being used despite clarifications by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the company. \par \par \uc2  MBf Holdings, the parent company of MBf Capital, called a news conference for 0600 GMT to reassure investors after a run on deposits on Wednesday, triggered by rumours -- emphatically denied by MBf -- the group's chief executive officer had died. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar traded in a subdued range with the focus on the ringgit/Sing cross. It has been weak since falling below the crucial 0.5000 level for the first time last week. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are looking at 0.4800 as the medium-term target for ringgit/Sing. But in the longer term, the fundamentals suggest it should be at 0.5200 or 0.5300," the European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah slipped to 3,022/27 from an opening 3,012/17 as ample money market liquidity spurred corporate dollar purchases, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A local bank dealer in Jakarta said the dollar appeared to be capped by selling out of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the 3,030 level. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in forward markets pulled the won back from a record low of 914.90 to the dollar, but there was still heavy importer dollar demand, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar remained rock solid around T$28.601/602 as market turnover soared on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's intensified defence of it after cutting commercial bank reserve requirements. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to US$87.793 billion at the end of August from US$88.77 billion a month before. Analysts attributed the drop to the bank's vigorous defence of the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt9p0219v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
