\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Down Mid-AM In Line With Other Regionals \b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1997\par 05:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (AP-Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar is lower against the U.S. dollar in mid-morning trading Wednesday, compared with its level in late Asian trading Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's fall is line with the decline Wednesday morning of other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar, under what market participants say is heavy selling by Hong Kong-based institutions. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar is stronger against the Malaysian ringgit, however. The ringgit's fall against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has outstripped that of the Singapore dollar, as market participants have sold the ringgit against the relative safe haven of the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the majority of trading in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  consists of selling directly against the U.S. dollar, say traders. \par \par \uc2  At 0300 GMT (11:00 PM EDT) the U.S. dollar is trading at S$1.5177, up from S$1.5140 in late Asian trading Tuesday and S$1.5055 early Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit meanwhile is at S$0.5037, down from S$0.5058 late Tuesday and below S$0.5100 early Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is likely to rise further against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say traders, should the ringgit continue to weaken. Few doubt the U.S. dollar will succeed in breaching S$1.5200, and traders at both domestic and foreign banks here are targeting a short-term level of S$1.5250 for the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Likewise, market participants expect the ringgit to fall to a level of S$0.4800 during Wednesday's trading, so bearish is sentiment towards the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  There remain some residual fears, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, the republic's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is unwilling to see the Singapore dollar strengthen against the ringgit to such an extent. Some market participants are nervous that the MAS will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, buying the ringgit against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar in order to keep the ringgit above S$0.5000. \par \par \uc2  In the Singapore money market, the overnight rate on Singapore dollar deposits is unchanged from late Tuesday at 2.875%-3.0%. \par \par \uc2  The three-month deposit rate also flat at 4.0%-4.125%. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 17-09-97 \par \par \uc2  0312GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt9h06vuz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
