\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS-Rupiah sheds gains, weighs on others. \b0\par\par\uc2 281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0315 GMT - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed early gains in thin trade as market absorbs impact of Indonesia's move to curb forward transactions. \par \par \uc2  * Rupiah slips to 2,937/47 against early high of 2,850 and opening at 2,860/90. Sharp drop in swap rates drives players to liquidate long rupiah positions. \par \par \uc2  * Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited forward selling against rupiah by domestic banks to foreign customers to $5 million. \par \par \uc2  * Malaysian ringgit eases in line with rupiah's fall, but closure of markets for holiday dampens interest. \par \par \uc2  * Ringgit quoted at 2.9270/300 to dollar against 2.9040/70 late on Friday. Still seen targeting 3.00 level, but fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near recent low of 2.9600 expected to provide some support. \par \par \uc2  * Singapore dollar probes downside but off Friday's lows as wariness of possible U.S. dollar sales by Brunei limits decline. Talk of dollar sales by Brunei government's investment arm spurred Sing dollar recovery from low of 1.5380 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  * Singapore dollar at 1.5140/50 against Friday's 1.5100/50. \par \par \uc2  * Thai baht still under pressure from concerns about domestic politics and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil. \par \par \uc2  * Philippine peso subsides from early highs, triggered by surge in domestic interest rates due to new liquidity reserves. \par \par \uc2  * Peso quoted at 30.38/43 against opening 30.25. \par \par \uc2  * Manila shares continue to haemorrhage with main stock market index slipping through 2,000 psychological support. \par \par \uc2  * 