\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Sturdy Singapore dollar turns volatile. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 1997\par 11:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 29 (Reuter) - The chaos rampaging through Southeast Asian markets pulled the usually unflappable Singapore dollar off its perch on Friday, before talk of heavy U.S. dollar sales by oil-rich Brunei brought it bouncing back. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell to a 38-month low of 1.5375 to the U.S. dollar in early trade as the Malaysian ringgit, the Thai baht and Indonesia's rupiah dived to uncharted depths. \par \par \uc2  But it staged a sharp rebound to 1.4960 in the afternoon on talk the Brunei Investment Agency (BIA) had sold U.S. dollars in large amounts for the Singapore dollar and Malaysian ringgit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar -- at 1.40 to the dollar in early January -- was at 1.5050/1.5100 at 0900 GMT and dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , long a regional safe haven in times of trouble, was likely to remain volatile. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated the BIA had sold at least $200-300 million against the Singapore dollar in thin Friday afternoon markets. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at a European bank said the BIA had sold around $150 million through his bank. The extent of the move suggested they had sold far more, probably through a number of channels, he added. \par \par \uc2  "We spoke with them this morning and they seem to be very concerned about the erosion of the Singapore dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Brunei dollar is pegged to Singapore's under a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  interchangeability agreement. \par \par \uc2  "Brunei has always been a net seller of dollars against the Singapore dollar even prior to the (regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) crisis," said Ishak Ismail, market intelligence analyst at independent research firm I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  "But the market was stunned because they don't normally enter in the ringgit," he said. \par \par \uc2  But the move was likely to provide little more than a temporary respite as investor confidence in Malaysia remained badly damaged after Kuala Lumpur's unexpected move on Wednesday to stop short-selling of major stocks, he said. \par \par \uc2  "For Singapore, it's more a competitive depreciation rather than anything else," he said. \par \par \uc2  A surge in Singapore's overnight interbank rate to highs of seven to eight percent, coupled with the speed of the Singapore dollar's recovery, sparked some speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market or squeezed short-term liquidity to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But most dealers remained unconvinced the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would try to defend its dollar aggressively in the middle of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turbulence. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it's the MAS. The ringgit/Sing is too low and they won't be happy with it continuing to depreciate because then the Sing would be too strong," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit has fallen to all-time lows against the Singapore dollar, in line with its almost eight percent depreciation against the U.S. dollar in the past week. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong added to the fray by saying central banks could not control exchange rates if the markets believed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were overvalued. That pulled the Singapore dollar off its afternoon highs. \par \par \uc2  "You can't fight the market," Goh told a news conference on the final day of a visit to Japan. \par \par \uc2  He said the Singapore dollar had lost only one percent of its value against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of major trading partners during the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis, despite falling five percent against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore Newsroom (65) 870-3080; Fax (65) 776-8112 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8t00kgf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
