\par\par \b \uc2  S.E. Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  show signs of divergence. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jacqueline Wong \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1997\par 06:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 25 (Reuter) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed more signs of divergence on Monday as several revived despite the Thai baht being sold down, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the baht eased on mounting perceptions that its international bailout package might not be sufficient and talk of renewed opposition planning for a vote of no-confidence against the government of Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh. \par \par \uc2  "People are shifting out of Thailand because the country's reserves look bad and its IMF (International Monetary Fund) loan may not be enough to bail it out of its economic troubles," a foreign dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar buying for loan repayments also affected the market and the baht drifted past the key 34 per dollar level to 34.05/15 on the onshore market at 0400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Offshore, the baht was at 33.550/750 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht would remain under pressure from heavy commercial demand for the U.S. unit to repay foreign loans. \par \par \uc2  Over the past few weeks, weakness in one Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spilled over into others as the market lumped them together. But that did not happen on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's rupiah was seen stable on relatively tight liquidity. Dealers said the market was cautious about selling it as the prospect of tight liquidity loomed with some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions last month maturing soon. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah was at 2,690/2,700, compared with an opening of 2,685/2,715 on Monday. It touched a high of 2,705 and low of 2,670. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit was rangebound with most of the action at midday centred on the ringgit/Sing cross, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There's some buying just below the 54 level coming mainly from long-term players," a Singapore-based dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was at 2.7710/40 to the dollar compared with its close of 2.7640/80 in Kuala Lumpur on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said comments over the weekend from U.S. financier George Soros denying involvement in Southeast Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, would lend psychological support. \par \par \uc2  Soros also said he believed stability would return soon and that some regional units might be due for a rebound. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar firmed moderately to 1.4968/78, inspired somewhat by its safe haven status in the wake of the baht's slide, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was still vulnerable to selling, but the pace of sales would slacken if markets remained wary of events in Thailand, prompting flight-to-quality buying, they said. \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, the won hit an all-time low despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It broke through 900 to the dollar and then slipped to a new low of 903.80. One \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 902. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso traded slightly weaker and looked likely to test 30 to the U.S. dollar, but banks were adopting a wait-and-see attitude on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The peso was quoted at 29.880/960 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar was barely changed with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showing no sign of easing its recent defence. It hovered around T$28.727 to the U.S. dollar, the level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has defended it from speculative attack. On Saturday, it closed at T$28.726. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8p00tne\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
