\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Singapore dollar weakness seen persisting. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1997\par 11:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 13 (Reuter) - The newly beleaguered Singapore dollar could be in for a spell of sustained weakness if the U.S. dollar stays bullish internationally and turmoil in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets persists, economists said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell to an early low of 1.5175 to the U.S. dollar, levels not seen since July 1994, before talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) near that level prompted a slight recovery. \par \par \uc2  It stood at 1.5140/50 at 0910 GMT, but \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers and analysts said it could slide towards the 1.53, and eventually, 1.55 levels. \par \par \uc2  The latest bout of selling of the usually sturdy Singapore dollar was attributed to an MAS official's comments that the current Singapore dollar level was appropriate to what was happening in the market. \par \par \uc2  The official of the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told reporters on Tuesday that the U.S. dollar had been rising in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and the Singapore dollar had depreciated by about five percent against the U.S. unit since the start of 1997. \par \par \uc2  Her comments sparked active sales of the Singapore dollar, pushing it abruptly through the psychological support level of 1.50 against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously, those comments gave the market a green light," said Daniel Lian, head of Asian bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at NatWest Markets. \par \par \uc2  But he said market players, particularly large hedge funds, had already been gearing up to test the 1.50 level. \par \par \uc2  "What the funds in New York are tracking very closely is our export numbers and whether we can convincingly tell them we're able to engineer a recovery in exports," Lian said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's non-oil exports, long used to double-digit growth, have been struggling with a downturn in global electronics demand since last year. \par \par \uc2  Non-oil domestic exports expanded by 4.4 percent in the second quarter of 1997 after contracting 4.1 percent in the previous quarter, according to the latest official figures. \par \par \uc2  Electronics account for around 70 percent of Singapore's non-oil exports. \par \par \uc2  Most economists said the Singapore dollar's weakness could help exports while having only a mild inflationary impact. \par \par \uc2  "Given the benign state of international price trends now, there's further potental for the Singapore dollar to weaken in the medium term without threatening a large rise in price instability," said Desmond Supple, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at BZW Asia. \par \par \uc2  He said Singapore's policy of managing its exchange rate on a trade-weighted basis made a weakening of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inevitable in the context of a robust U.S. dollar and declines in neigbouring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the effective devaluations of the Thai baht and Philippine peso last month. \par \par \uc2  "We could quite easily see the weakness extending to the 1.55 levels further down the line. And I wouldn't view that as a sign the MAS was diluting its commitment to keeping inflation low," Supple said. \par \par \uc2  But Jacqueline Ong, economist at independent research house I.D.E.A., said a decline to the 1.55 level would be overdone in view of Singapore's sound fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "On a trade-weighted basis, levels from 1.48 to 1.51 may be a little bit painful in terms of making imports more expensive," Ong said. \par \par \uc2  "If we're talking about prolonged weakness, I think 1.50 is already the limit," she added. \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore Newsroom (65) 870-3080; Fax (65) 776-8112 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8d017f1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
