\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - RUPIAH UNDER SIEGE, BI HIKES RATES. \b0\par\par\uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 13 (Reuter) - The Indonesian rupiah fell prey to the malaise afflicting Southeast Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, tumbling to a historic low before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided some support by raising rates. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was quoted at 2,665/75 at 0420 GMT after plunging to a low of 2,679 in morning trade against an opening 2,674/76. The rupiah's previous low was 2,665 on July 21. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the slide to lingering spillover weakness from other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to a local newspaper report quoting an economist as saying Bank Indonesia should allow the rupiah to find its own level. \par \par \uc2  Sjahrir, an economist and brokerage firm owner, was quoted in the Kompas daily as saying the market would ultimately decide a fair value for the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  "According to me, just let the rupiah fall to 2,700 or 2,800. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not have to do anything. Once they (speculators) realise the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't done anything, the dollar is already overvalued," Sjahrir was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  Singapore dealers said there were market rumours the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might widen or remove its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band for the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah received a brief lift after Bank Indonesia raised interest rates for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  certificates (SBIs) of all maturities by 100 basis points. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the move, which followed a 50-basis-point cut in SBI rates for paper above one week last Friday, was inevitable in the face of the rupiah's slide and could be a prelude to the possible removal of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar remained under pressure but recouped some of its losses on talk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near its low of 1.5175 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The domestic unit was quoted at 1.5127/37 at 0420 GMT and dealers said the U.S. dollar continued to draw strong bids near the low end of 1.5100. \par \par \uc2  They said local banks were seen selling the U.S. dollar near its highs earlier, often an indication of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's presence, but no confirmation was available. \par \par \uc2  "The market still seems to be quite biddish on the U.S. dollar," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A European bank dealer said the U.S. dollar would be bought on any dips to 1.5100 or 1.5050 for an eventual move up to 1.53. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit moved narrowly but showed persistent signs of weakness and dealers said the market was likely to take a crack at the 2.80 level, a low not seen since the ringgit was officially floated in 1973. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/ringgit could still reach 2.80 and probably go above that level," said a Malaysian bank dealer. "But I'm expecting some resistance at 2.7850." \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was quoted at 2.7720/50 to the dollar at 0420 GMT against 2.7645/75 at the close of Kuala Lumpur trade on Tuesday. It hit a new 42-month low of 2.7930 earlier on Tuesday. The Thai baht opened weaker in domestic markets as players returned from a one-day holiday and bought the dollar on concern about weakness in regional markets. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 31.59/64 to the dollar in onshore markets and at 31.10/20 in offshore markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were some sales of dollars offshore ahead of the expected mid-August maturity of short baht positions, established when the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under speculative attack three months ago. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso remained weak as banks bought up the dollar in volatile regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Corporate demand for the dollar also weighed on the peso. \par \par \uc2  The peso was at 28.925/29.050 against its previous close at 28.66. Manila traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have sold dollars at the 28.70/28.78 levels earlier but no confirmation was available from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar was weak but its decline was expected to be slowed by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tight liquidity in domestic money markets. \par \par \uc2  It was at T$28.723/726 against Tuesday's T$28.718 close. \par \par \uc2  -- Syed Azman in Kuala Lumpur (603-206 2323), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221 384-6364), Anuchit Nguyen in Bangkok (662) 652-0642, Manila newsroom (632-841-8937), Taipei newsroom (8862-508-0815) and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703090) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8d01793\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
