\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Sharply Lower Late Against U.S. Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 1997\par 12:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (AP-Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was again sharply lower against the U.S. dollar at the close of Asian trading Thursday, as commercial interests, investors and speculative traders all sought to buy the U.S. dollar against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In afternoon trading the U.S. dollar breached resistance at S$1.4750 to hit S$1.4773 at 1000 GMT (6:00 a.m. EDT), up from S$1.4740 around 0140 GMT (9:40 p.m. EDT), and higher than S$1.4690 in early morning dealings. \par \par \uc2  Few traders were prepared to call a short-term top for the U.S. dollar against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and most fully expect further falls Friday and in the following week. \par \par \uc2  The latest fall in the Singapore dollar was sparked by comments made by Richard Hu, Singapore's finance minister. Speaking in Singapore's parliament Wednesday afternoon, Hu said the Singapore dollar's exchange rate is determined by market forces, and the recent weakness in the Thai baht and the Philippine peso 'should not pose a significant threat to (Singapore's) competitiveness.' \par \par \uc2  The market interpreted the remarks as an indication that the Singapore government was prepared to allow the Singapore dollar to weaken against the U.S. dollar in line with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent depreciation. \par \par \uc2  With the threat of MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen lifted from the market, U.S. funds and banks, as well as domestic banks and corporations, were all seen buying the U.S. dollar against Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the ringgit is at S$0.5580, up from S$0.5566 earlier and S$0.5550 in late trading Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Local money market rates have not changed since late Wednesday despite the Singapore dollar's weakness against the U.S. dollar. The overnight rate on Singapore dollar deposits is unchanged from late Wednesday at 4%-4.125%. Three-month interbank rates are also steady at 4.125%-4.375%. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 24-07-97 \par \par \uc2  1049GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7o05h49\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
