\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Regionals post further losses. \b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 1997\par 11:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 18 (Reuter) - Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  racked up further losses in Friday afternoon trade as funds continued to pile into the U.S. dollar in a market rife with rumours. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said liquidity remained very low and spreads wide, exaggerating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  "Liquidity continues to be a problem so it takes just a few (million) dollars and this market can push it around," a treasury manager at a U.S. bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  He said there was talk of Asian central banks gearing up for concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of heavy speculative attacks on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the de facto devaluations of the Thai baht and Philippine peso earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  "There have been all sorts of rumours going through the markets. My own opinion is it could just be people caught short trying to cover back," he added. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury and the International Monetary Fund are on high alert over financial turmoil in emerging markets worldwide, which has sent stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunging, the Washington Post reported on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The newspaper quoted officials as saying they were worried the turmoil could slow growth in some of the world's most vibrant economies, which have become key markets for U.S. exports. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia, the ringgit remained weak after sinking to a fresh three-year low of around 2.6080 to the U.S. dollar on selling by offshore funds. \par \par \uc2  It was quoted at 2.6060/90 at 0845 GMT against 2.5970/6000 around noon. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the apparent absence of Bank Negara Malaysia from the market, despite talk it had checked rates around the 2.6000 level earlier, contributed to the ringgit's slide. \par \par \uc2  Another senior trader at a Singapore-based bank said dollar/ringgit could face some resistance around 2.6120. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit has fallen more than three percent this week partly due to a perceived change in stance by Malaysian authorities towards allowing market forces to determine the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar recovered some equilibrium after sinking to a new 30-month low of around 1.4665 against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide caused players to turn cautious, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We heard the MAS were up at the 1.4635 level but there's no confirmation," a dealer said. "Ultimately, I don't believe the MAS is too worried about dollar/Sing at these levels." \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was quoted unchanged from its noon levels of 1.4605/15 at 0845 GMT, against an early high of 1.4550/60. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the traditionally robust \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was being affected by declines in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a perception the MAS would condone some weakness in it. \par \par \uc2  "The MAS will look at it in terms of percentages with the neighbours," a dealer said, referring to the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of monitoring the Singapore dollar against a trade weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They would be uncomfortable if all the neighbours weakened and the Sing dollar did not move," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah fell to a new low of 2,502 to the dollar on spillover weakness. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had earlier recovered briefly after falling to a low of 2,497 as offshore players sold it during the Jakarta lunch break. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian President Suharto was quoted as saying he had asked the country's top finance officials to monitor the situation and take steps against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, the baht continued to weaken on commercial dollar demand before a long weekend but its slide was relatively muted, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 30.350/450 to the dollar onshore at 0830 GMT and 30.300/400 offshore. \par \par \uc2  A Singapore-based dealer said dollar/baht was likely to move up slowly towards the 30.50 level, with buying interest expected below 30.00. \par \par \uc2  The market showed little immediate reaction to news that Japan and Thailand had agreed to cooperate to promote stability in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, using measures including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -- Syed Azman in Kuala Lumpur (603-206 2323), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221 384-6364), Anchalee Koetsawang in Bangkok (662) 652-0642 and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703090) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt7i02h62\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
