\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr/Short-Term -2: Economists Mixed On Direction \b0\par\par\uc2 236 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 1997\par 12:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  However, the unexpected weakness in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days has divided economists on exactly how much weaker the Singapore dollar will get in the medium-term and what sort of recovery it will make by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  An average forecast based on a poll of 13 economists by Dow Jones indicates the U.S. dollar will have retreated again, to reach S$1.4340 by the end of the year. The forecasts range from S$1.4100 to S$1.4500. \par \par \uc2  'The Singapore dollar can go down quite (a bit) lower ... even lower than S$1.48. This is because of the short term sentiment and it has nothing to do with the fundamentals,' said the chief economist at a Singapore bank. \par \par \uc2  Other differ. 'It's quite unlikely for the (Singapore) dollar to go (lower) to S$1.48,' says J.P. Morgan economist Ong Sin Beng. 'The Singapore dollar is underpinned by strong fundamentals,' he says. \par \par \uc2  'If you look around the region, we're the only country with a strong current account surplus. You don't see that elsewhere,' Ong says. \par \par \uc2  Market analysts agree that one major uncertainty is whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the city-state's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current weakness. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 18-07-97 \par \par \uc2  1052GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7i05c99\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pic