\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore dollar slips as speculators move in. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 8 (Reuter) - The Singapore dollar continued to head south against its U.S. counterpart on Tuesday largely on the back of sales by U.S. funds, blamed for the recent sell-off in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The funds are trying to attack all the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region," a dealer with a U.S. bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  He said the rest of the market was mostly squared up in the Singapore dollar but U.S. funds were still extending their long dollar/Sing positions. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was quoted at 1.4386/96 to the U.S. dollar at 0820 GMT against its opening 1.4358. It briefly hit a low of 1.4405 on selling from London-based parties. \par \par \uc2  The domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under siege since late last week after Thailand's shock move towards a managed float exchange rate system. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said large U.S. funds, which had bet on a devaluation of the Thai baht, were turning on other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to test how far they could be pushed. \par \par \uc2  "I think dollar/Sing can go to its last high of 1.4500/20," the U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  He added that most players were not afraid of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of that level, unless the Singapore dollar moved sharply. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) typically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem sudden movements in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Jacqueline Ong, economist at research firm I.D.E.A., said the Singapore dollar's traditional "safe haven" status among regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have undergone a slight shift with Singapore authorities endorsing a more modest appreciation of the unit. \par \par \uc2  "In the past, we've always assumed a strong Sing dollar policy from the government. But recently (Finance Minister) Richard Hu said there's no need for the Sing dollar to appreciate that much if inflation stays under control," Ong said. \par \par \uc2  "To the extent that foreign inflationary pressures become more muted, our exchange rate does not have to appreciate as much as it did before," Hu said in a speech in March. \par \par \uc2  Ong said players were unlikely to go aggressively into the Singapore dollar despite regional uncertainties due to a belief the MAS would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent its sharp rise. \par \par \uc2  "Although we're sure now that the local authorities don't want the Sing dollar to be strengthening so fast, they definitely do not want it to weaken to like 1.45 overnight," she said. \par \par \uc2  She said a gradual move in the Singapore dollar towards 1.45 would probably be acceptable to local authorities, as long as the U.S. dollar also strengthened towards 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore newsroom (65-8703090) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt7802yhq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
