\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand Baht Stabilizes Offshore After Nearly 14% Plunge \b0\par\par\uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1997\par 09:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (AP-Dow Jones)--The Thai baht has stabilized in early afternoon trading in the offshore market after earlier falling by nearly 14% against the U.S. dollar on the news that the Bank of Thailand is abandoning its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg in favor of a managed float system. \par \par \uc2  Offshore trading is fitful with bid/offer spreads as wide as 2% to 3%. Onshore trading activity, meanwhile, is completely non-existent, say traders at Thai banks, with spreads up to 10% being quoted. \par \par \uc2  At 0715 GMT (3:15 a.m. EDT) the dollar is quoted at 28.75 baht in the offshore market, relatively little changed from levels around 28.50 baht at 0130 GMT, but 16% above its level in offshore trading late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  At the same time swap rates in the offshore market are down sharply after traders sought to dump baht and square their positions, a move which allows hedge fund speculators to unwind open long dollar forward positions at a profit, according to traders. \par \par \uc2  One month forwards are quoted at a premium of 40 satang compared to 75 satang Tuesday, while the three month forward premium is at 120 satang down from 225 satang Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  'It will take the market time to adjust to developments. At the moment no one wants to be in the market. Everyone is just waiting for London to come in to see what happens then,' said one regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Singapore bank. \par \par \uc2  Liquidity in the offshore market is still extremely restricted, however, prompting one senior trader at a Thai bank offshore to forecast that the baht will rally from present levels on scarcity of supply. \par \par \uc2  The prohibition on domestic Thai banks providing baht to foreign participants unless they can demonstrate an underlying business transaction remain in force, despite the jettisoning of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. \par \par \uc2  The details of how the Bank of Thailand is to implement its new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy are yet to be made fully clear, but the Thai trader expects a 'Singapore-type system.' \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore actively manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's level against an undisclosed basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain what it sees as acceptable levels. \par \par \uc2  The Thai trader estimates that the Bank of Thailand is targeting a ceiling for the U.S. dollar of between 30 baht and 31 baht. \par \par \uc2  'Any higher than that and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market,' he said. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 02-07-97 \par \par \uc2  0736GMT \par \par \uc2  1028 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7204w00\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
