\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Baht dips as overseas demand eases. \b0\par\par\uc2 887 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1997\par 07:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  * Thai baht eases as offshore demand subsides. Some bargain hunting interest in dollar seen at current levels. \par \par \uc2  * Singapore dollar firmer but off its highs on perceived \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after heavy overnight sales of Malaysian ringgit/Sing dollar cross. \par \par \uc2  * Malaysian ringgit slightly firmer on dollar sales by exporters and corporate interests. \par \par \uc2  * Indonesian rupiah steady as market seeks new leads with liquidity conditions providing sole point of interest. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 10 (Reuter) - The Thai baht shed some of its recent strength on Tuesday as demand abated in offshore markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar selling (for baht) in offshore markets has eased. Some offshore players think it's an opportunity to buy the dollar at the current rate," a foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "We may see a base forming for the dollar just below 24.00 baht for now," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Bangkok dealers said there was less selling pressure on the dollar in offshore markets as some foreign investors had acquired baht to cover short positions by selling Thai stocks late last week. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's two-tier baht system, imposed around mid-May to cut off baht supply to speculators, was expected to remain in place for a few months. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Thailand (BOT) told  local brokerages informally not to handle big lot stock transactions with foreigners in its latest move to cut offshore players' access to the baht, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Senior BOT officials were not immediately available for comment. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 24.200/24.300 to the dollar at 0435 GMT in offshore markets against 24.150/24.250 late on Monday. In the domestic market, the baht was traded at 25.730/25.735 against 25.740/25.750 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The BOT lowered the baht/dollar midrate to 25.72 from 25.75 on Monday as the dollar weakened against the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was off its highs, partly due to perceived \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after the domestic unit shot up against the U.S. dollar on heavy hedge fund sales of the ringgit/Sing cross overnight. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/Sing traded to as low as 1.4170 last night. But this morning the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be around," the U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Local banks have been bidding aggressively around the 1.4200 level so we should see it remaining supported," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , typically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets through major local banks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the ringgit/Sing cross had fallen to an overnight low of 0.5635 as hedge funds, which were long, unloaded their positions. \par \par \uc2  "There was a big sell order on ringgit/Sing in London. When that happened dollar/Sing collapsed and dollar/ringgit shot up," a European bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The cross was quoted at 0.5663/70 at 0435 GMT and was seen trading within a new range of 0.5650-0.5675 for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit firmed slightly against the U.S. dollar but dealers said its gains were limited by selling interest in the ringgit/Sing cross and activity was confined to a narrow range. \par \par \uc2  Another Singapore-based European bank dealer said there were some Malaysian exporters selling dollars at 2.5090 and speculators were picking up dollars around 2.5070. \par \par \uc2  "If the 2.5110 level holds for the next couple of days, then we should see dollar/ringgit moving down," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  He said the selling of dollar/Sing was mainly by U.S. funds, which had built up positions in anticipation of the New York Commodities Exchange (NYCE) launching futures contracts on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The NYCE said on Monday it was delaying the launch of a Sing dollar futures contract, partly due to expressions of concern from the MAS. \par \par \uc2  "This is causing many to dump dollar/Sing," he said. \par \par \uc2  Futures contracts on the Thai baht, ringgit and Indonesian rupiah are expected to be launched on June 26. \par \par \uc2  Singapore has long restricted internationalisation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keeps a tight rein on trading of the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah was relatively steady amid a dearth of fresh leads after the country's May 29 general election. \par \par \uc2  A U.S. bank dealer in Jakarta said offshore operators were still long on rupiah in the forward market although there were attempts to square positions at any opportunity. \par \par \uc2  She said many operators were also seen trying to establish fresh outright positions, keeping the market in range trading. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah was at 2,428.50/29.50 at 0435 GMT, compared with an opening of 2,429.0/29.5. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is mixed. There is no clear trend yet," a Japanese bank dealer said, adding that the market was desperate for fresh news after slowing down sharply in the past few days. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said rupiah liquidity was a bit tight, with overnight money traded at 12.0/13.0 percent, but attention was focused on the liquidity outlook for Friday. \par \par \uc2  -- Madhav Reddy in Kuala Lumpur (603-206 2323), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221 384-6364), Anuchit Nguyen in Bangkok (662) 652-0642 and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703090) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt6a04bhy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
